# Custom Directives

## Overview

Your order Agent must email the customer when an order is placed. If the
Action calls the mailer, a Turn that fails later still sends the email for
an order that never committed. A custom Directive fixes the order of
events: the Action returns the email as data, Jido validates it before
commit, and the owning Plugin sends it after commit.

In this Livebook you'll build a notifier Plugin. When it's done, a placed
order produces this delivery, stamped with the revision it belongs to:

```text
%{to: "ada@example.com", message: "Order A-100 confirmed", revision: 1}
```

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Pieces

| Piece | Invoked | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `use Jido.Agent.Directive` | — | Makes a struct a Directive with a `validate/1` callback. |
| `validate/1` | Before commit, for every returned Directive | Accepts or rejects the Directive. |
| `directives/1` | When Jido normalizes the Plugin | Claims Directive types for this Plugin. |
| `dispatch/4` | After commit, once per owned Directive, in order | Performs the Directive. |
| `prepare_dispatch/4` | Before each outbound Signal leaves | Adjusts the Signal. |
| `directive_handler:` | After commit, for owned Directives with no `dispatch/4` | Server-level fallback. |

Each custom Directive type has exactly one owning Plugin. A Directive with no
owner fails the Turn before commit with `"Agent Directive has no owner"`.

## Define the Directive

`use Jido.Agent.Directive` adds a default `validate/1`. When the module
defines `schema/0`, the default validates the struct against it.

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Notify do
  use Jido.Agent.Directive

  @schema Zoi.struct(
            __MODULE__,
            %{
              to: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.min(1),
              message: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.min(1)
            },
            coerce: true
          )

  @enforce_keys Zoi.Struct.enforce_keys(@schema)
  defstruct Zoi.Struct.struct_fields(@schema)

  def schema, do: @schema
end
```

Override `validate/1` for rules a schema can't express. It must return
`{:ok, directive}` with the same struct type, or `{:error, reason}`.

## Define the Outbox

The notifier needs somewhere to deliver. This runtime records deliveries. In
your application it's the mailer, queue, or webhook client.

```elixir
{:ok, _registry} = Registry.start_link(keys: :unique, name: GuidePlugins.Registry)

defmodule GuidePlugins.Outbox do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(init) do
    name = {:via, Registry, {GuidePlugins.Registry, {:outbox, init.agent_id}}}
    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, [], name: name)
  end

  def deliveries(agent_id) do
    [{pid, _}] = Registry.lookup(GuidePlugins.Registry, {:outbox, agent_id})
    GenServer.call(pid, :deliveries)
  end

  @impl true
  def init(deliveries), do: {:ok, deliveries}

  @impl true
  def handle_call({:deliver, delivery}, _from, deliveries),
    do: {:reply, :ok, deliveries ++ [delivery]}

  def handle_call(:deliveries, _from, deliveries), do: {:reply, deliveries, deliveries}
end
```

## Define the Plugin

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Notifier do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def directives(_opts), do: [GuidePlugins.Notify]

  @impl true
  def child_spec(init), do: Supervisor.child_spec({GuidePlugins.Outbox, init}, id: __MODULE__)

  @impl true
  def dispatch(outbox, %GuidePlugins.Notify{} = notify, context, _opts) do
    GenServer.call(
      outbox,
      {:deliver, %{to: notify.to, message: notify.message, revision: context.state_version}}
    )
  end

  @impl true
  def prepare_dispatch(_outbox, signal, context, _opts) do
    {:ok, %{signal | subject: "agents/" <> context.agent_id}}
  end
end
```

- `directives/1` claims `Notify`. No other Plugin can claim it, and no Plugin
  can claim a built-in Directive.
- `dispatch/4` receives the runtime PID, the validated Directive, and a
  `Jido.Plugin.DirectiveContext`: Turn ID, Agent ID, source Signal, committed
  revision, this Plugin's committed state, and the caller's Turn context.
- `prepare_dispatch/4` sees every outbound Signal from `Emit`, `AgentSend`,
  and `OwnerSend`, with a `Jido.Plugin.SignalContext` that includes the
  target.

## Define the Agent

The notebook attaches the Plugin with `extensions:`, because a Livebook cell
has no source file for `plugin` discovery.

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Orders do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "orders",
    extensions: [GuidePlugins.Notifier]

  agent do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{placed: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/custom-directives")

    route "order.place", as: :place do
      action %{order_id: order_id, email: email},
        name: "place_order",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), email: Zoi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | placed: context.agent_state.placed ++ [order_id]},
         [
           %GuidePlugins.Notify{to: email, message: "Order #{order_id} confirmed"},
           emit("order.placed", %{order_id: order_id})
         ]}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

The Action returns two Directives: one custom, one built-in. Neither runs
inside the Action.

## Evaluate Without Effects

`cmd/3` returns the Directives as data:

```elixir
agent = GuidePlugins.Orders.instantiate!(id: "orders-1")
place = GuidePlugins.Orders.place_signal!(%{order_id: "A-100", email: "ada@example.com"})

{:ok, candidate, [notify, emit]} = GuidePlugins.Orders.cmd(agent, place)
{candidate.state.placed, notify, emit.signal.type}
```

The result is:

```text
{["A-100"],
 %GuidePlugins.Notify{to: "ada@example.com", message: "Order A-100 confirmed"},
 "order.placed"}
```

Nothing was sent. In a test, you assert on this tuple.

## Invalid Directives

Validation runs before commit. An empty address fails the Turn:

```elixir
bad = GuidePlugins.Orders.place_signal!(%{order_id: "A-101", email: ""})
{:error, errors} = GuidePlugins.Orders.cmd(agent, bad)
Enum.map(errors, & &1.__struct__)
```

The result is `[Zoi.Error]`. The default `validate/1` returns the raw Zoi
error list, not a `Jido.Error` struct. The Agent state doesn't change.

## Deliver After Commit

Run it live. `default_dispatch:` sends emitted Signals to this notebook:

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

{:ok, orders} =
  Jido.start_agent(GuidePlugins.Orders,
    id: "orders-1",
    default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(orders)
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(orders, place)
committed.state.placed
```

The result is `["A-100"]`. After the commit, the Server ran the Directives in
order: `dispatch/4` delivered `Notify`, then `Emit` sent the Signal through
`prepare_dispatch/4`.

```elixir
GuidePlugins.Outbox.deliveries("orders-1")
```

The result is
`[%{to: "ada@example.com", message: "Order A-100 confirmed", revision: 1}]`.

```elixir
receive do
  {:signal, %Jido.Signal{type: "order.placed"} = signal} -> {signal.subject, signal.data}
after
  1_000 -> :no_signal
end
```

The result is `{"agents/orders-1", %{order_id: "A-100"}}`. The Plugin set the
subject on the way out.

## When Dispatch Fails

Commit doesn't guarantee delivery. If `dispatch/4` returns an error or times
out, the Server stops the rest of the batch. The commit and the success reply
stand. `directive_timeout:` limits the whole batch. The default is 5,000 ms.
`Jido.AgentServer.status/2` reports the result in `last_effect_outcome`.

For work that must eventually happen, make delivery idempotent and track
pending work in the Plugin's owned state.

## Handler Fallback

A Plugin that owns a Directive only to reduce state has no `dispatch/4`. By
default, the Server does nothing with that Directive after commit. To run
it, start the Server with `directive_handler:`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
Jido.start_agent(MyApp.Agent,
  directive_handler: fn directive, context ->
    MyApp.Effects.run(directive, context.agent, context.state_version)
  end
)
```

The handler receives the Directive and a context with the committed Agent,
revision, Server PID, instance, partition, Turn ID, and source Signal ID. It
returns `:ok` or `{:error, reason}`. A Plugin's `dispatch/4` always takes
precedence.

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("orders-1")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- A custom Directive is a struct with `use Jido.Agent.Directive`.
- `directives/1` claims Directive types. Each type has one owner.
- Jido validates every Directive before commit.
- `dispatch/4` performs owned Directives after commit.
- `prepare_dispatch/4` adjusts outbound Signals.
- `directive_handler:` runs owned Directives that have no dispatcher.

